Task: Resolver los toques de la rejilla con `UIViewRepresentable` (Fase 4, pattern-copy_20260910)

The grid stops being sixteen Buttons and becomes sixteen drawn cells with one
touch layer over them. `PatternTouchLayer` wraps a `TouchGridView` with
`isMultipleTouchEnabled`, and `touchesBegan/Ended/Cancelled` map each touch to a
slot index.

- A touch belongs to the cell it went down on. Lifting somewhere else cancels it
  rather than moving it — what a Button already did, written out (FR21).
- Origins are keyed by `UITouch` identity, not by cell, because two fingers can
  land on the same cell and UIKit guarantees the touch object lives for the whole
  touch.
- Cell frames are measured, not derived: each cell reports its own frame through
  a `PatternCellFrames` preference key in a named coordinate space. Deriving the
  grid from four columns and eight points of spacing would work today and break
  silently the day the spacing changes.
- The pressed highlight is drawn by hand — `Brutalist.pressedOpacity`, new —
  because `.buttonStyle(.plain)` gave it for free and its absence reads as a grid
  that does not respond.
- Accessibility is kept explicitly: each cell is still an element with the button
  trait, the selected one carries `.isSelected`, and an accessibility action
  selects it.

Behaviour is unchanged in this commit: a touch still selects on the way up. The
chord is wired in the next task.

